{
  "slug": "artist:the-specials",
  "name": "The Specials",
  "editorial_article": "Formed in 1977 in Coventry, England, The Specials emerged as architects of the 2 tone movement, blending ska and rocksteady rhythms with the raw energy of punk. Founded by Jerry Dammers, the group debuted with the 1979 single 'Gangsters,' which reached the UK Top 10 after receiving airplay on John Peel's BBC Radio One show. Their self-titled 1979 debut album was produced by Elvis Costello and featured horn arrangements by Dick Cuthell and Rico Rodriguez. The band became known for their mod-inspired rude boy style and explicit political stances, notably with the 1981 recession-themed No. 1 single 'Ghost Town.' While the original lineup fractured in 1981, with Terry Hall, Neville Staple, and Lynval Golding departing to form Fun Boy Three, the band reunited in various capacities between 1993 and 2022. Their 2019 album 'Encore' marked a return to the top of the UK Albums Chart, ending a 39-year wait for a number-one studio record.",
